      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To study effects of 6-month physical training on some parameters of hemostasis in patients with coronary heart disease and non-insulin dependent diabetes (NIDDM)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Long-term physical training resulted in lowering of fibrinogen level and increase of fibrinolytic activity assessed as euglobulin fraction clot lysis time, while factor VII activity remained unchanged. These changes were accompanied with elevation of total and high density lipoprotein cholesterol levels without any shifts in low density lipoprotein cholesterol and triglycerides. Most pronounced positive effect physical training exerted on results of a test with standard single fat load, which was used as a model of postprandial lipemia. Serum cortisol level significantly decreased after 6-month training, especially after fat load and subsequent physical exertion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In patients with combination of coronary heart disease and NIDDM long-term physical training was associated with decreased thrombogenicity of blood."
        }
      ]},
